WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.915
Idea. Think of a chocolate bar. You might see an advert for it on television, along with millions of other people. Or someone might hand you a free sample in a supermarket.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:19.616
Both are promotion. Promotion means communicating with customers to make them aware of a product and persuade them to buy it.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:41.101
Method. Step one: classify the method. Above the line uses mass media to reach a large audience: television, radio, newspapers, posters and billboards. Below the line is more direct and targeted: special offers, coupons, free samples, sponsorship, leaflets and loyalty schemes.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:54.253
Two methods need a closer look. Sponsorship means paying towards an event so that the business's name is shown. A product trial lets customers try before they buy, and gives the business feedback.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:01:06.998
Step two: match the method to the customers and the budget. A national brand can afford television to reach millions. A local café is better with cheaper leaflets for people nearby.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:19.773
Step three: link the method to awareness or persuasion, then to sales. Add a limitation if needed: it costs money, the effect may be short, and there is no guarantee people will buy.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:32.493
Worked example. Here is a real question from Paper two in November 2021. Explain one advantage for a business of using sponsorship. It is worth three marks.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:37.635
Mark one is the advantage: sponsorship increases brand awareness.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:48.492
Mark two explains how. The business's name and logo are shown to everyone at the event or watching it, which could be a local, national or global audience.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:58.147
Mark three is the result. People who follow the event become familiar with the brand and may choose to buy from it, which increases sales revenue.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:08.635
Common mistake. Mistake one: putting special offers or sponsorship above the line. They are direct and targeted, so they are below the line.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.503
Mistake two: jumping straight to more sales. Show how customers are reached first.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:31.840
Your turn. Now you try. A new bike repair shop has a small budget and wants local customers. Should it use a national television advert or leaflets delivered to nearby homes? Classify each method, then choose one. Pause and decide.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:48.925
Answer. Television is above the line; leaflets are below the line. Choose the leaflets. They are cheaper and reach people who live close enough to bring their bikes in, so more local customers visit. A drawback: some leaflets will be thrown away unread.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:03:00.079
That is the method for this type: classify the method, match it to the customers and budget, then link it to awareness and sales. Now try the practice questions.
